---
// The appendix: backup slides for questions, reachable only by `a`, a slide number or the index. Each is a question,
// three to five bullets from the thesis, and where in the thesis it is. No names of jury members anywhere here. A
// bullet writes its formulas as LaTeX between dollars.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import { texify } from './tex';
import clamp from '../../data/defence/clamp.json';
const cs = clamp.summary;
const qs: { id: string; q: string; bullets: string[]; ref: string }[] = [
  { id: 'q1', q: 'What single question unifies the thesis?', ref: 'Ch. 1 §1.3, Ch. 11', bullets: [
    'How much does a simple local rule tell you about the global behaviour it produces, and what does the behaviour tell you about the rule and the network: genotype against phenotype.',
    'Three objectives: rigorous foundations for these systems; how network topology shapes the dynamics; the systems turned into instruments, for neural networks and for network science.',
    'In the thesis’s words: to map, understand and apply the link between iterating simple local rules and the rich landscape of emergent outcomes.',
    'Chapters 4 to 10 each answer one part; Chapter 11 puts the answers side by side.' ] },
  { id: 'q2', q: 'What are the main limitations?', ref: 'Ch. 11 §11.3', bullets: [
    'The theory is shallow: exact for a small corner of rules, one step deep elsewhere; the rest is simulation.',
    'Narrow settings, broad conclusions: one resolution, one network family, one starting density, synthetic networks only.',
    'Evaluation lagged behind generation: point estimates without error bars, no rule held out, an importance score whose reliability went unmeasured.',
    'Mechanisms are proposed, not shown: that sensitive rules synchronise and jagged rules discriminate is a reading of a pattern.' ] },
  { id: 'q3', q: 'Why these networks and these parameter ranges?', ref: 'Ch. 3', bullets: [
    'A Life-like rule only reads the share of a node’s neighbours that are on, so what matters is how the degrees are spread and how the links are arranged.',
    'Each family is a dial for one of those: rewiring $p$ runs a ring or grid from clustered and long to random and short; the power $\\alpha$ runs preferential attachment from no hubs to one hub.',
    'At $p = 0$ the rings and grids are the neighbourhoods of classical cellular automata, which anchors each dial in known territory.',
    'Synthetic networks only: empirical ones are the next step.' ] },
  { id: 'q4', q: 'Is your Lyapunov exponent really an entropy?', ref: 'Ch. 4', bullets: [
    'It is a tangent-space quantity: the growth rate of a count of walks along which a defect can travel, which makes it entropy-like by construction.',
    'For the parity rule on a graph the exponents are $\\ln\\lvert\\lambda_k(A) + a_\\circ\\rvert$, the maximal one $\\ln \\rho(A + a_\\circ I)$; for the rule without self, the logarithms of the adjacency eigenvalues.',
    'The thesis itself calls it closer to an entropy than to a rate of separation.',
    'Wolfram (1984) introduced both notions for cellular automata side by side.' ] },
  { id: 'q5', q: 'Without a chaos claim, why keep the name “Lyapunov spectrum”?', ref: 'Ch. 4 §4.4', bullets: [
    'For continuity with the literature; the thesis says plainly that it counts walks and claims no chaos.',
    'What the chapter adds: exact spectra as benchmarks, which correct spectra published earlier.',
    'And the Boolean Jacobian, whose expected row sum is the sensitivity that Chs. 5 and 6 use to read rules.',
    'Still, the chapter stands somewhat apart from the rest of the thesis.' ] },
  { id: 'q6', q: 'Why odd resolutions and mirrored intervals?', ref: 'Ch. 5 §5.2, App. C', bullets: [
    'Complementation symmetry: swapping on and off must map the rule space onto itself.',
    'That needs the cut of the density axis mirrored around $\\tfrac12$, with a middle interval that contains $\\tfrac12$, hence an odd number of intervals.' ] },
  { id: 'q7', q: 'How good is the mean-field approximation?', ref: 'Ch. 5 §5.3', bullets: [
    'It is one step deep and annealed: every round, contacts are redrawn at random.',
    'It ignores correlations and clustering, which the real network builds up at once.',
    'Hence simulation remains necessary; the genotype narrows the search, it does not replace it.' ] },
  { id: 'q8', q: 'Is the genotype filter really cheaper than a genetic algorithm?', ref: 'Ch. 6 §6.1.3', bullets: [
    'The genetic algorithm of Das et al. spends some $10^7$ to $10^8$ simulations on the search.',
    'The filter simulates nothing: a bitwise condition and 512 mean-field curves, in a fraction of a second.',
    'Verification costs both the same order: the saving is in the search.',
    'The evolved rules of Das et al. synchronise better, on lattices: $\\phi^9_{23,47}$ is not the best synchroniser available, and the rule spaces differ.' ] },
  { id: 'q9', q: 'Does small-world structure cause synchronisation?', ref: 'Ch. 3 §3.3.2, Ch. 6 §6.1.3', bullets: [
    'No causal claim. The rule fails for $p \\le 0.04$, peaks near $p \\approx 0.3$ (94%), beyond the small-world band, and drops to 39% at $p = 1$.',
    'Short paths look necessary but not sufficient, for this rule.',
    'Rewired grids behave differently: 82% unrewired, above 95% for $0.02 \\le p \\le 0.4$.',
    'At $p = 0.2$ the thesis reports 83% from the screening and 78% from the sweep, both at half the nodes on: two estimates of one point.',
    '$p$ is not the same amount of rewiring in both families: a ring changes $2p - p^2$ of its links, a grid $p$. The text now says so; the simulations stand, only the comparison between the two $p$ axes shifts.' ] },
  { id: 'q10', q: 'Can the network’s spectrum predict where the synchroniser works, as for oscillators?', ref: 'Chs. 4, 6, 11', bullets: [
    'Not studied.',
    'The spectrum governs the dynamics exactly only for affine rules such as parity; $\\phi^9_{23,47}$ is not affine.',
    'The closest bridge is pinning control, which Ch. 11 names as a direction.' ] },
  { id: 'q11', q: 'Can a classifier discover behaviour classes, or only transfer known ones?', ref: 'Ch. 8', bullets: [
    'Its output only transfers the labels it was trained on.',
    'Its internal representation can support exploration without labels, but that is not discovery.',
    'A self-supervised attempt turned out hard.' ] },
  { id: 'q12', q: 'How much confidence do the 98% and 61% deserve?', ref: 'Ch. 8', bullets: [
    'The test set was kept apart for the final numbers, but the validation set chose the augmentations.',
    'Each entry is the best of a few runs, as its caption says; there are no error bars.',
    'No rule was held out, so 61% measures recognising rules the network has seen.',
    'The lesson does not depend on the decimals: first check whether scanning the rule table already solves the task.' ] },
  { id: 'q13', q: 'What makes the best classification rules special? The edge of chaos?', ref: 'Ch. 9 §9.4.2, §9.6.3, §9.7', bullets: [
    'The analysis works at the level of partitions of the rule space by jaggedness, not rule by rule; the best individual rules were not examined one by one.',
    'Calling the rules in the sweet spot “complex”, or placing them at an edge of chaos, is a hypothesis; no measure of their behaviour was computed to test it.',
    'The edge of chaos is contested anyway: Mitchell, Hraber and Crutchfield (1993) could not reproduce Packard’s 1988 result.',
    'Part of it may be counting: partitions are largest near a jaggedness of $\\tfrac12$, and the best of many rules looks better than the best of few.' ] },
  { id: 'q14', q: 'Why not graph neural networks or graph kernels for classification?', ref: 'Ch. 9 §9.7', bullets: [
    'Beating them was not the aim; on social and chemical benchmarks they do better.',
    'The rule-based pipeline needs no node attributes, handles networks of any size, and comes with one interpretable parameter: the rule.' ] },
  { id: 'q15', q: 'What did the joint doctorate with São Paulo bring?', ref: 'Chs. 9, 10', bullets: [
    'Network automata as instruments: the pipeline that classifies networks by the patterns a rule draws on them comes from the São Paulo group.',
    'In Ch. 9, first-authored by Lucas de Oliveira, my part is the link from jaggedness to the genotype measures of Ch. 5, and review and editing.',
    'Ch. 10, on node importance, grew out of the same collaboration.' ] },
  { id: 'q16', q: 'Isn’t the stubborn person just a zealot from the voter model?', ref: 'Ch. 3 §3.5, Ch. 10', bullets: [
    'Related, and the thesis says so: a zealot never changes its mind, and neither does a clamped node.',
    'But the aim differs: not a model of opinions, a probe of how much each node matters.',
    'Every node is clamped in turn: an intervention, not one zealot observed.',
    'The rule is deterministic, non-monotone and chosen by its genotype. A quantitative comparison with zealot results is not in the thesis; it would be the natural test.' ] },
  { id: 'q17', q: 'Clamping is an intervention; what does merely swapping states do?', ref: 'Ch. 10 §10.3.1', bullets: [
    'Swap the most central person who is up with the least central one who is down, again and again.',
    'On preferential-attachment networks with 8 contacts: at most 10 swaps by closeness flip the outcome 90% of the time.',
    'Random swaps only push the outcome towards a coin toss: the 90% mark is never reached.',
    'That random reference covers one setting only, $\\alpha = 0$, reconstructed from saved, binned output: thin, and a full rerun is the next step.',
    'Clamping needs no such control: it tests every node, blind to any centrality.' ] },
  { id: 'q18', q: 'Is dynamical importance confounded by conditioning on consensus?', ref: 'Ch. 10 §10.2.2, §10.4, Tab. 11.1', bullets: [
    '$\\eta$ pools clampings that change a node’s start (its hand was up) with ones that only confirm it (already down).',
    'Which starts reach consensus is itself linked to centrality; the thesis does not separate the two.',
    `On the toy network of tonight, the Spearman correlation of $\\eta$ and degree is $${cs.spearmanEtaDegree.toFixed(2)}$ overall, $${cs.spearmanGenuineDegree.toFixed(2)}$ from the changing clampings only, $${cs.spearmanConfirmatoryDegree.toFixed(2)}$ from the confirming ones only.`,
    'So the ranking survives the split here, on one toy network; that is an illustration, not a result.',
    'Why not split the thesis’s own scores? The raw clamping outputs are no longer available, so it needs a rerun; it is listed as the next step.' ] },
  { id: 'q19', q: 'Could the score find keystone species, or whom to vaccinate?', ref: 'Chs. 10, 11', bullets: [
    'It needs a known network and a dynamics that can be simulated on it.',
    'The automaton is a generic probe, not a model of the real process: whether its ranking carries over is exactly the untested step.',
    'Choosing a set of nodes rather than ranking single ones is influence maximisation, the direction Ch. 11 proposes.' ] },
  { id: 'q20', q: 'Why discrete automata, and what is lost against continuous models?', ref: 'Ch. 1, MACCA-GW', bullets: [
    'Gained: exact computation with no discretisation error, and networks, where there is no continuum to discretise.',
    'Lost: calibration to physical parameters and built-in conservation laws.',
    'The groundwater model sits right on that trade-off.' ] },
  { id: 'q21', q: 'Why is the groundwater work not in the thesis?', ref: 'MACCA-GW', bullets: [
    'It is a spatially explicit cellular-automaton model of groundwater flow, not a network automaton.',
    'It does not fit the thesis’s story from rules to networks, so it is published separately, in the ACRI 2026 proceedings.',
    'Next to MODFLOW it offers simplicity and many runs at once for ensembles; not parallelism as such, since parallel MODFLOW 6 exists.' ] },
  { id: 'q22', q: 'With one more year, what would you do, and what differently?', ref: 'Ch. 10 §10.4, Ch. 11', bullets: [
    'Finish Ch. 10 properly: faster simulation, the two kinds of clamping split, the score’s reliability measured, a comparison with an epidemic or Ising dynamics, empirical networks.',
    'Differently from day one: held-out test sets, and every raw output stored.' ] },
  { id: 'q23', q: 'What do cellular automata offer interpretability research?', ref: 'Chs. 7, 8', bullets: [
    'Circuits whose weights are known exactly: a cellular automaton written into a neural network by hand (Ch. 7).',
    'The shortcut lesson: high accuracy that came from reading the rule off the first row, not from the behaviour (Ch. 8).',
    'An idea for future work, not a result of the thesis.',
    'It is also a deliberate change of field, not only a bridge.' ] },
];
---
<Slide label="Backup slides" appendix id="backup" class="bk">
  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <ol class="index">
    {qs.map((x) => <li><a href={`#${x.id}`} data-go={x.id}>{x.q}</a></li>)}
  </ol>
</Slide>
{qs.map((x) => (
  <Slide label={x.q} appendix id={x.id} class="bk">
    <h2 class="bq">{x.q}</h2>
    <ul class="bl">{x.bullets.map((b) => <li set:html={texify(b)} />)}</ul>
    <p class="cite">{x.ref} · <a href="#backup" data-go="backup">back to the questions</a></p>
  </Slide>
))}

<style>
  .index { columns: 2; column-gap: calc(60 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(29 * var(--u)); display: block; margin-top: calc(10 * var(--u)); }
  .index li { break-inside: avoid; margin-bottom: calc(12 * var(--u)); }
  .index a, .cite a { color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; }
  .index a:hover, .cite a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .bq { font-size: calc(56 * var(--u)); max-width: calc(1600 * var(--u)); }
  .bl { font-size: calc(38 * var(--u)); gap: calc(26 * var(--u)); max-width: calc(1600 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(20 * var(--u)); }
</style>
